# Contributing to {{project_name}}

This is the per-change workflow. Local + CI gates are aligned, so a green
local check normally predicts a green CI run.

---

## One-time setup (fresh clone)

```bash
cd /path/to/{{project_name}}
make install        {% if language == 'python' %}# creates venv if missing, installs deps
{% elif language == 'nodejs' %}# runs `npm install`; Husky's prepare script arms hooks
{% elif language == 'go' %}# go mod download + installs gofumpt + golangci-lint into ./bin/
{% endif -%}
make install-hooks  # {% if language == 'python' %}registers pre-commit + pre-push git hooks
{% elif language == 'nodejs' %}re-arms Husky hooks idempotently (defensive)
{% elif language == 'go' %}sets core.hooksPath = hooks (native git hooks)
{% endif %}
```

{% if language == 'python' -%}
`make install-hooks` is separate so `make install` is safe in non-git
tempdirs (CI runners, smoke tests). On a git-initialised clone, also run
`make install-hooks` once to wire up:

- **pre-commit** — runs `ruff check --fix` + `ruff format` on every commit.
  If ruff modifies a file, the commit fails; `git add` the fixes and re-commit.
- **pre-push** — runs the full `pytest` suite. Blocks pushes that would
  fail CI on the same suite.

Both hooks live in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and are versioned, so other
clones get the same hooks after `make install-hooks`.
{% elif language == 'nodejs' -%}
Husky's `"prepare": "husky"` script in `package.json` runs during
`npm install` and arms hooks via `core.hooksPath = .husky`. So
`make install` alone usually suffices. `make install-hooks` is the
defensive idempotent re-arm path for users who ran
`npm install --ignore-scripts` (which some CI configs do for security).

Two hooks land in `.husky/` and are versioned (checked into the repo):

- **pre-commit** — runs `lint-staged`, which invokes `biome check --write`
  on staged `.ts` / `.tsx` / `.json` files. Auto-fix triggers a failed
  commit; `git add` the fixed files and re-commit.
- **pre-push** — runs the full vitest suite. Blocks pushes that would
  fail CI on the same suite.

The `.husky/_/` directory Husky creates at runtime is gitignored
(third-party output; not user-edited).
{% elif language == 'go' -%}
Native git hooks via `core.hooksPath`. `make install-hooks` runs
`git config core.hooksPath hooks`; the hook scripts at `hooks/pre-commit`
and `hooks/pre-push` are versioned in the repo.

- **pre-commit** — runs `./bin/gofumpt` (detects unformatted files,
  rewrites them, and exits non-zero so the commit is blocked; `git add`
  the fixes and re-commit) + `./bin/golangci-lint run` (incremental on
  existing HEAD, full on greenfield).
- **pre-push** — runs `go test ./...`.

Hook framework choice: native git, no `pre-commit` framework, no Husky.
No Python or Node dependency for Go install/check/hooks paths. (The
plan-review Makefile targets `review-plan-by-codex` /
`review-plan-by-claude` still need `python3` because the env scrubber
is a Python script — that's a cross-language dep documented in
`docs/usage.md`.)
{%- endif %}

### Codex CLI (required for `make review-plan-by-codex`)

```bash
# Install (one-time; see https://developers.openai.com/codex):
brew install --cask codex     # or per the official docs

# Log in (one-time; opens browser):
codex login

# Verify:
command -v codex && codex --version
```

### Claude CLI (required for `make review-plan-by-claude`)

```bash
# Install (one-time):
npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code   # or per the official docs

# Log in:
claude login

# Verify:
command -v claude && claude --version
```

If either CLI is unavailable on a contributor's machine, the corresponding
`make review-plan-by-*` target exits cleanly with an install hint rather
than failing silently.
{% if github_review_mode in ['claude', 'both-docs'] %}
### After your first push to GitHub (required for `claude[bot]` PR review)

The `.github/workflows/claude-review.yml` workflow needs an OAuth token to
post reviews as `claude[bot]`. Without this secret the workflow runs but
the action exits with an auth failure — no review is posted.

One-time setup, right after you create the GitHub repo and push:

1. **Install the Claude Code GitHub App** on your account (one-time per
   user, not per repo): https://github.com/apps/claude
2. **Generate the OAuth token locally** (one-time per user; opens browser):
   ```bash
   claude setup-token
   ```
3. **Copy the token** Claude prints, then add it as a repo secret:
   `Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → New repository secret`
   - Name: `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`
   - Value: paste the token

**The same token works across multiple repos** — it's per-user, not
per-repo. If you already have it set on another repo, you can copy that
value directly. Generating a new token does NOT invalidate older ones;
they coexist until explicitly revoked.

Verify by opening any PR after this is set — `claude[bot]` should comment
within ~1 minute.
{% endif %}
